this morning we're focusing on major  crises that teenagers and children face  according to the cdc the proportion of  mental health related emergency  department visits rose more than 30  percent in kids 12 to 17 years old  during the first months of the pandemic  in 2020 and there was a more than 50  percent increase in emergency department  visits for suspected suicide attempts  among adolescent girls during one month  period in 2021 compared with 2019. jamie  howard is a senior clinical psychologist  from the child mind institute a  non-profit dedicated to children's  mental health good morning how you doing  good morning thanks for having me of  course of course so what are some  warning signs that parents can look out  for when it comes to depression in  children and teens  with depression what we want to look for  is some mood changes like depression  shows sadness and irritability  irritability more than sadness sometimes  with kids so that's something to keep in  mind changes in eating or sleeping and  loss of interest or motivation and doing  things that you used to enjoy doing  you know  i want to ask about irritability because  it seems like yeah teenagers are  always irritated with something  oftentimes parents because we don't know  what we're talking about what's the  difference between a teenager just being  a teenager and then as a parent thinking  you know i need to dig a little bit  deeper into what's going on right so  there's usually some functional  impairment which means when kids are  really irritable they're like pushing  friends away they're pushing family away  it's not just a momentary kind of thing  it's kind of sustained and frequent  let's talk about anxiety as well because  in addition to depression anxiety is  racking kids in this generation what are  some of the warning signs to look for  there  so with anxiety what we want to look for  is sudden onset of avoidance of things  that kids usually did sort of  comfortably right like going to school  suddenly they don't want to go to school  and it's unclear why and they might be  really sort of rigid about not wanting  to do that maybe that's something like  social anxiety they're suddenly afraid  of being embarrassed we also want to  look for changes in our in our physical  body so like increased heart rate  nauseousness sort of like complaints  about our physical wellness that's  another tricky one for adolescents right  because one day they wake up and they  may not feel good in that shirt anymore  or whatever so how do you know when it's  a crisis when it's just run-of-the-mill  adolescent bumps so again like with all  illnesses what we want to look for is  this functional impairment so kids need  to go to school so if the anxiety is  keeping them from going to school that's  a very important job that they have  that's when we want to say we have to  help you with this because you need to  function you need to be able to get out  the door and do your important things  what's your advice  jamie to parents who may can be  concerned about their kids  who they think could be contemplating  suicide or who appear to be very  depressed  what should parents do or say in that  situation so you want to ask very  directly are you thinking about hurting  yourself are you thinking about killing  yourself that always seems awkward  though because you think that are you  planting a seed or is it  it seems like an um  very volatile question to ask right but  all the experts we've talked to say you  have to be that direct you want to be  really direct and you want to say it in  a calm empathic way so it's not like an  accusation it's a it's an extension of a  conversation i've noticed you haven't  been feeling like so good lately you  haven't seemed like yourself and then  you open up a conversation have you been  thinking about killing yourself is that  part of it and then if the child says  yes but just sometimes it feels like it  would be easier not to be here that's  what we call suicidal ideation and you  want to get your child to a mental  health practitioner right away if they  say yes and i have a plan do you want to  ask that's the second question do you  have a plan have you thought about what  you would do then that becomes more of  an emergency and we want to contact  9-1-1 potentially if they have a plan  and a desire a strong intention to die  you can also call the national suicide  helpline 273 talk because they can help  you sort that out if you're having a  hard time as a parent are there certain  ages that you think are more vulnerable  or more  susceptible to this is it boys or girls  or is there an age it's girls and  teenagers are more likely to die by  suicide and the rates are going up  steadily over the past decade and a half  even before the pandemic why is that why  is that girls i mean  so there's there's a few different  hypotheses about it part of it is the  developing brain and the changes with  onset of puberty  and part of it is a sociological  explanation of some of the pressures of  being a young girl yeah um i'll take  this moment to have um  some transparency full transparency on  the show after you visiting us um i told  myself i'm gonna go home and i'm gonna  ask a very direct question which is  about suicide to my kids which we  communicate a lot but it's a question  that i've never asked so um i walked in  my house my heart was racing like never  before i mean i played in football games  we're on this show we have some very  high stakes moments um i've never had  more anxiety or nervousness um as a  parent so i walked up to my kids and i  went down the line and they all answered  very honestly and my daughter said  something that uh she never said to me  before she said you know  i've never thought about committing  suicide but i noticed when my brothers  are injured because of sports they get  more attention and i didn't know that  and  i bring this up because if we wouldn't  have had this conversation i wouldn't  have walked in and learned that small  detail about my daughter and having a  little bit more peace of mind knowing my  daughter doesn't have suicidal thoughts  um so for parents who might be nervous  about that first conversation because  for a lot of parents it is the first  conversation how do they hype themselves  up to do it so you don't have to  actually feel calm you just kind of want  to fake it as best you can that's good  advice yeah and you can rehearse it  ahead of time  the script take some some deep breaths  right like slow steady belly breathing  actually really helps to reduce our  anxiety and then you can have an opener  like you know i was watching the news  this morning and these people were  talking about this and i thought well i  haven't asked you and then you can use  that as the opener  hey that's great yeah thank you so much  jamie we appreciate it advice that is  very good information all right if you  or someone you know is seeking mental  health resources you can call the  national alliance on mental illness  helpline at 1-800-950-620  in a crisis you can text nami to 741-741

